  "slug": "deleterious-evolutionary-insights-76b1",
  "summary": "New simulations and a review link deleterious and standing variation to genetic load, climate adaptation limits, gene drive outcomes, and crop breeding.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how the distribution of fitness-relevant variation, including deleterious alleles, constrains evolutionary and applied outcomes. A review covers identification, cross-species variation, and persistence of deleterious mutations, with implications for crop breeding and conservation. Forward simulations indicate that reproductive competition amplifies purifying selection and reduces genetic load more efficiently than the comparison scenario. Individual-based mosquito models show that standing genetic variation evolves in response to a homing suppression drive and alters drive conversion and resistance outcomes. In Boechera stricta, genome-environment analyses identify climate-linked SNPs, suggesting that hot, dry projected climates may exceed adaptive capacity. In admixed crop germplasm, simulations show that low founder isoeliteness reduces genomic selection gain, which an isoeliteness-guided method addresses. Most evidence here is simulation-based or preprint-stage.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
